Licence of this repository, and of the binaries it produces
===========================================================

TWO DIFFERENT THINGS, DO NOT CONFLATE THEM.


1. THE BINARIES THIS REPOSITORY PRODUCES
----------------------------------------

    GPL-2.0-or-later.

FFmpeg is configured here with --enable-gpl and statically links x264 and
x265, both of which are GPL. That makes the resulting ffmpeg, ffprobe and
libav*/libsw* binaries GPL-2.0-or-later.

--enable-version3 is deliberately NOT used, so the result stays at "version 2
or later" rather than being forced up to GPLv3. --enable-nonfree is never
used; the result would be undistributable.

If you redistribute those binaries you must pass on the licence texts (every
archive ships them in licenses/) and you must provide the complete
corresponding source. That source is published with every release as
tas-ffmpeg-<version>-corresponding-source.tar.xz, and it contains the
unmodified FFmpeg tarball, every dependency at its pinned commit, and this
entire build system.

See docs/LICENSING.md.in for the full component table and the written offer.


2. THE BUILD SYSTEM IN THIS REPOSITORY
--------------------------------------

    MIT.

versions.lock, flags/, scripts/, docker/, docs/ and .github/ are original
work. They are not derived from FFmpeg or from any GPL dependency; they
configure and invoke those projects, which is not the same thing.

GPL-2.0 section 3 requires "the scripts used to control compilation and
installation" to ACCOMPANY the binaries. It does not require them to be
licensed GPL. MIT is GPL-compatible, so this build system is included
verbatim in every corresponding-source archive and that obligation is fully
satisfied — while leaving the scripts reusable in contexts the GPL would
forbid. This mirrors BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds, which is likewise MIT.

